"""Writes docs/VALIDATION_1998.md from the 1998 tables and validation_summary_1998.json."""
import pandas as pd, json
def fmt(d): return ', '.join(f'{k} {v:,}' for k,v in d.items())
OUT='output/'
A=pd.read_csv(OUT+'crosswalk_tehsil_1998_to_2017.csv'); C=pd.read_csv(OUT+'crosswalk_tehsil_1998_to_2023.csv')
rep=json.load(open(OUT+'validation_summary_1998.json')); r=rep['tehsil_1998_to_2017']
m98=pd.read_csv('build/intermediate/unit_master_1998_solved_frame.csv')
L=[]
L.append('# Validation report: 1998 layer (Census 1998 -> 2017, and chained 1998 -> 2023)\n')
L.append('Built 3 November 2026. Scope: Punjab, Sindh, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa (with the former FATA) and Balochistan. The 2017 -> 2023 layer has its own report in VALIDATION.md.\n')
L.append('## 1. The 1998 frame')
L.append(f"The 1998 sub-district units come from PBS/PCO Census Publication 161, *Population of Administrative Units 1951-98* (Data Darbar table census_admin_units_1951_1998). The lowest published tier is used: tehsils, talukas and sub-tehsils; a sub-division only where it has no tehsil rows; each Frontier Region as one unit. That gives {len(pd.read_csv(OUT+'unit_master_1998.csv'))} units in 114 districts and agencies, and they sum exactly to the published district populations and to the five provincial totals (Punjab 73,621,290; Sindh 30,439,893; NWFP/KP 17,743,645; Balochistan 6,565,885; FATA 3,176,331).\n")
L.append('For three districts the 1951-98 report does not list the 1998 census tehsils but the towns and cantonments created under the 2001 local government system (Karachi 24 units, Lahore 7, Peshawar 5). These are not 1998 census units, so the 1998 side is collapsed to the whole district there: Karachi City District (one unit, as Professor Khan asked), Lahore City District (one unit) and Peshawar District (one unit). Peshawar loses nothing (the whole district was one tehsil in 2017, restated 1998 population 2,026,851 = the 1998 district count). Lahore loses the 1998 split between Lahore City and Lahore Cantt tehsils; it can be restored from the 1998 District Census Report of Lahore if needed. After the collapse the 1998 frame has 424 units.\n')
L.append('One more thing to know about this frame: the 1951-98 report presents the 1998 counts on the administrative map as it stood when the report was compiled (about 2000-01), not strictly as on census day. Umerkot, a district from 1993 to December 2000 and again from 2004, is shown inside Mirpur Khas; the report\'s Karachi, Lahore and Peshawar towns are the 2001 units already mentioned. So "1998 frame" in these tables means the frame of Census Publication 161, and a 1998-vintage dataset that uses Umerkot as a district should be mapped to Mirpur Khas first (Umerkot, Kunri, Samaro and Pithoro talukas) or joined at taluka level.\n')
L.append('## 2. Completeness')
L.append(f"- 1998 frame: {r['units_1998']} of {r['units_1998_in_frame']} units appear in the crosswalk.")
L.append(f"- 2017 frame: {r['units_2017']} of {r['units_2017_in_frame']} sub-district units appear.")
L.append(f"- Districts: 114 of 114 (1998) and {rep['district_1998_to_2017']['districts_2017']} of 134 (2017; Islamabad excluded).")
L.append(f"- Rows: {r['rows']} tehsil rows 1998 -> 2017; {rep['tehsil_1998_to_2023']['rows']} tehsil rows 1998 -> 2023 (chained); {rep['district_1998_to_2017']['rows']} and {rep['district_1998_to_2023']['rows']} district rows.\n")
L.append('## 3. Weight checks')
L.append(f"- Population weights sum to 1 for every 1998 unit (max deviation {r['pop_weight_sum_max_abs_dev']:.1e}); area weights likewise ({r['area_weight_sum_max_abs_dev']:.1e}). Same for the chained 1998 -> 2023 table.\n")
L.append('## 4. Conservation')
L.append(f"Pushing the 1998 population of every 1998 unit through the weights reproduces, for every 2017 unit and every 2017 district, the \"POPULATION 1998\" column PBS printed in Census 2017 Table 1 (max deviation {r['tehsil_conservation_max_abs_dev']} persons). Provincial totals:\n")
L.append('| Province | Crosswalk | PBS Census 2017 Table 1, Population 1998 | PCO 1951-98 report |'); L.append('|---|---|---|---|')
for p,v in rep['provincial_1998_totals'].items(): L.append(f"| {p} | {v['crosswalk']:,} | {v['pbs_2017_table1_restated']:,} | {v['pco_1998_report']:,} |")
L.append('\nFor the chained 1998 -> 2023 table there is no PBS figure to check against (PBS never printed 1998 populations on 2023 units); the check is that the national total is conserved to the person (131,547,044) and that every 2023 unit is reached.\n')
L.append('## 5. How each unit was matched')
L.append('The same idea as the 2017 -> 2023 layer, written as one procedure for all district groups. Districts are first grouped (a namesake district plus any new district whose restated 1998 population equals the residual of a parent, verified by exact balancing; e.g. Chiniot 965,124 = Jhang 2,834,545 minus 1,869,421). Inside each group the flows from 1998 units to 2017 units are chosen to reproduce both the 1998 populations and PBS\'s restated 1998 populations exactly while opening as few unexpected cells as possible: same-named pairs cost almost nothing, a pair whose 2017 unit touches the polygon of the 1998 unit\'s namesake costs more, a non-adjacent pair more still. This is a fixed-charge transportation problem, solved exactly (scipy MILP). Two safeguards: a same-named pair with identical population in both tables is treated as unchanged, and where the district-level flows are unambiguous they are imposed on the tehsil-level solve (e.g. Kasur -> Lahore exactly 21,369 persons). Each solution is then tested for uniqueness by forbidding every opened cell in turn; where an equally cheap alternative exists the rows are marked medium confidence.\n')
L.append(f"- Methods: {fmt(r['method'])}.")
L.append(f"- Confidence: {fmt(r['confidence'])} (1998 -> 2017); chained 1998 -> 2023 inherits the lower of the two layers: {fmt(rep['tehsil_1998_to_2023']['confidence'])}.")
L.append(f"- {r['split_1998_units']} 1998 units map to more than one 2017 unit; {r['units_2017_with_multiple_sources']} 2017 units draw from more than one 1998 unit.\n")
L.append('Name spellings differ between the 1951-98 report and the 2017 tables (Buleda / Bulaida, Besham / Bisham, Kutmandai / Kot Mandai, Nurpur / Noorpur, Sobhodhero / Sobho Dero, Tandlianwala / Tandlian Wala, Kallar Sayyadan / Kallar Sayaddan). These were matched with a tolerant string comparison (shared stem and at least 70 percent similarity) within the district group, and every such pair is then confirmed by the population balance; the six pairs matched purely by population balance (one_to_one_renamed) are listed in section 9.\n')
L.append('## 6. Medium-confidence rows (1998 -> 2017)')
L.append('| district_1998 | unit_1998 | pop_1998 | unit_2017 | flow_1998 | note |'); L.append('|---|---|---|---|---|---|')
for _,x in A[A.confidence=='medium'].iterrows(): L.append(f"| {x.district_1998} | {x.unit_1998} | {x.pop_1998:,} | {x.unit_2017} | {x.flow_1998:,} | {x.note} |")
L.append('\nThe Dir case: the 1998 Lower Dir sub-divisions (Timargara, Jandul) and Upper Dir (Dir, Wari) became seven sub-divisions in 2017, 61,407 persons moved from Upper to Lower Dir, and the tables alone cannot say which 1998 sub-division Lal Qila and Samarbagh were cut from. The Mirpur Khas case: Sindhri taluka (150,628 in 1998 terms) draws from Mirpur Khas taluka and from Khipro (Sanghar); the share of Mirpur Khas is an inference.\n')
ru=A.drop_duplicates('unit_id_1998'); ok=ru.area_1998_pco_km2.notna()&(ru.area_1998_pco_km2>0); ratio=(ru.area_1998_implied_km2/ru.area_1998_pco_km2)[ok]
L.append('## 7. Area weights')
L.append(f"Area weights use PBS\'s published 2017 areas (Census 2017 Table 1): area of the 2017 unit times the share of its restated 1998 population that came from the 1998 unit. Rows by basis: {fmt(r['area_basis'])}. PBS printed no sub-district areas for Kohistan and Killa Saifullah in 2017; there the 2017 unit\'s area is the one implied by the 2023 tables through the 2017 -> 2023 crosswalk (flagged implied_2017_area_uniform_density). As a check, the area of each 1998 unit implied by the crosswalk is compared with the area printed in the 1951-98 report: the median ratio is {ratio.median():.2f} and {int(ratio.between(0.9,1.1).sum())} of the {int(ok.sum())} units with a printed 1998 area agree within 10 percent (columns area_1998_implied_km2 and area_1998_pco_km2). The disagreements are mostly in Balochistan and FATA, where the published areas themselves changed between reports.\n")
L.append('## 8. Open items')
L.append('1. Lahore 1998: the City / Cantt tehsil split is not in the 1951-98 report; one unit for now.')
L.append('2. The 1998 Karachi towns are 2001 units; Karachi is one unit as agreed.')
L.append('3. The uniform-density assumption in area weights applies to rows where a 2017 unit has more than one 1998 source (89 rows); a PBS 1998 or 2017 tehsil shapefile would replace it with measurement.')
L.append('4. The chained 1998 -> 2023 weights multiply two PBS-based layers; a row is only as good as the weaker layer, which the confidence column records.\n')
L.append('## 9. Every split, merge and renamed row (1998 -> 2017)')
L.append('| district_group | unit_1998 | pop_1998 | unit_2017 | pop_1998_restated_2017 | flow_1998 | pop_weight | area_weight | method | confidence |'); L.append('|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|')
S=A[(A.groupby('unit_id_1998').unit_id_1998.transform('size')>1)|(A.groupby('unit_id_2017').unit_id_2017.transform('size')>1)|(A.method=='one_to_one_renamed')].sort_values(['district_group','unit_1998','unit_2017'])
for _,x in S.iterrows(): L.append(f"| {x.district_group} | {x.unit_1998} | {x.pop_1998:,} | {x.unit_2017} | {x.pop_1998_restated_2017:,} | {x.flow_1998:,} | {x.pop_weight:.4f} | {x.area_weight:.4f} | {x.method} | {x.confidence} |")
open('docs/VALIDATION_1998.md','w').write('\n'.join(L)+'\n')
print('wrote docs/VALIDATION_1998.md', len(L),'lines')
